ed players, researchers are finding that the early stages of brain changes can begin even in high school and college athletes. This can lead to mood swings, impulse control issues, and cognitive decline—all of which would ruin a student's academic performance. **Reframing the Stereotype** Your point actually provides a perfect explanation for *why* so many people see football players struggling in class. However, it also shifts the perspective. If a player is struggling because their brain is physically healing from repeated trauma, it doesn't mean they are inherently "dumb" or lack natural intelligence. It means they are dealing with a neurological injury. If a track star breaks their leg, we don't say they are a naturally terrible runner; we recognize they are injured. Similarly, when a football player struggles with math after taking hits to the head all week, it is often a medical and physical issue, not a sign that they aren't smart. So, you are completely right. The physical toll of the game—specifically head trauma—is a massive, undeniable reason why some football players have a terribly hard time keeping up in the classroom.
